  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 204 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nA few areas of vorticity will approach South Texas today through \nMonday as they ride around the periphery of mid-level high pressure. \nThis, combined with PWAT values 1.9-2.1\", will allow for the \ndevelopment of showers and thunderstorms today through Monday. For \ntoday, have a low mention of PoPs (~15%), mainly along the afternoon \nseabreeze. Chances increase Sunday and Monday afternoons to low to \nmoderate (20-40%) as moisture increases and the area of vorticity \nmoves closer to the area. These chances will mainly remain over the \nCoastal Plains, with most of the area remaining dry. By Tuesday, \nhigh pressure begins to settle in more firmly over the area, \neffectively ending our chances for precipitation through the \nupcoming week. \n\nTemperatures will continue to be hot. Heat index values today and \nSunday will generally be 105-109; while a few locations could reach \n110-112, will not issue a Heat Advisory for this today since the \nconditions are not expected to be widespread. Heat index values \nincrease Monday through next week, with Heat Advisories likely each \nday. The WBGT risks will be high to extreme across South Texas \nthrough the forecast period. Please remember to take the necessary \nprecautions if you are working, playing, or practicing outside: \nLimit exposure to 1-2 hours, with at least 4 breaks/hour (~20 \nminutes total rest), limit your activity levels, drink plenty of \nwater, and wear light, loose clothing. \n\nLook before you lock!",
